Linux glibc baseline: a .so binds to the glibc symbol versions of the host that linked it. A build
on ubuntu-latest (26.04, glibc 2.43) fails to load on Ubuntu 24.04 and older ("version
`GLIBC_2.43' not found": sqrtf, remainderf), so the workflow builds Linux inside an ubuntu:22.04
container (glibc 2.35, the oldest Linux Unity 6 players support) and fails the job if the result
needs anything newer. Build locally on Ubuntu 24.04 or older for the same reason.

Reproducible Windows link: link.exe stamps the build time into the PE header and export table, so
the glue CMake project links with /Brepro (content hash instead). Unchanged sources therefore give
byte-identical binaries on every platform and the workflow's commit job has nothing to commit.

Unity glue (glue/box3d_unity_glue.c, b3u_* exports, GLUE VERSION 2): compiled into every binary
via glue/CMakeLists.txt since 2026-09-01. Version 2 (2026-11-07) widens b3u_RayHit's point to b3Pos;
the single-precision layout is unchanged, so only BOX3D_DOUBLE builds require it (the C# side
logs an error for an older _d library). The C# Burst paths (queries, events, body accessors)
require the glue and World.Create probes b3u_GlueVersion at startup. Every shipped binary above
carries it; a binary built without it logs the stale-glue error and the rerouted APIs throw
EntryPointNotFoundException.

On version bumps: update this file, rebuild ALL platforms (run the build-natives workflow),
regenerate bindings, run struct-size tests.
